{"type":"after","callId":"call@143","endTime":47511.876,"result":{"value":{"s":"\n    Home/Manual/Developer reference/Transport, framing & safety\n    \n\nTransport, framing & safety\nHow you reach the gun, what a frame looks like, and why nothing here can brick one\n✅bench👥communityLast verified 2026-08-27View as Markdown\nWhat the badges mean✅bench Verified on our bench📖official Official Battle Company docs🔍app Decoded from the Callsign app👥community Community-reported🧪software Built + software-tested📐spec Specified only🚧under construction Under constructionNothing unconfirmed is published — see Credits & sourcing.\n\nHeadline: \"One text protocol, three ways in.\" Sub: The BRX speaks a plain ASCII, comma-delimited command language on a hardware UART. Gen1 exposes it over Bluetooth Classic, Gen2/3 over BLE, and the community drives it from a wire — the frames are identical on all three. Background: DEV-01.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §1, §7c\nWho found this.Protocol discovery for the BRX platform is the work of LaserTagMods (JEDGE / JBOX). This page restates their findings independently, with our own bench verification noted per row.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md (header, §7d), README.md\nTransport by generation\n\n\nGeneration\nLink\nSpeed\nHow you connect\nConfidence\n\n\n\nGen1\nBluetooth Classic (SPP)\n57600 baud\nPair an HC-05 module (PIN , master role). The headset must be connected for Bluetooth to function.\n👥community\n\n\nGen2/3\nBLE — Nordic UART Service (NUS)\nUART bridge at 115200 behind the radio\nConnect from any BLE central: laptop (bleak), ESP32, phone. No pairing/PIN.\n✅bench\n\n\nAny\nHardware UART inside the gun\n115200\nWhat JEDGE drives directly (). No external accessory port exists on the BRX — a wired tap means opening the gun. Untested by us.\n👥community\n\n\nAny\nMicro-USB \"Programing Port\"\nUSB CDC (baud ignored)\nNot the  protocol — a separate / console. See the Serial console page.\n✅bench\n\n\n✅bench/\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSource: protocol/brx-protocol.md §1, §7c\nBLE — Nordic UART Service UUIDsService: RX characteristic (write to tagger): TX characteristic (notify from tagger): Advertised name:  (the last two bytes of the BLE MAC). The NUS service UUID is present in the advertisement, so scan-time generation detection works.ATT MTU negotiates to 23 bytes — chunk writes to ~20-byte payloads; this is required, not defensive. →  round trip ≈ 59 ms over BLE.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §1, §7a, §7b; mcp/brx_mcp/protocol.py\nGeneration detection heuristic.Power on the tagger and run a BLE scan. If it advertises the UART service → Gen2/3. If nothing appears on BLE but the device pairs over Bluetooth Classic → Gen1.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §1\nDEV-02diagram pendingLink topology: host ↔ BLE NUS ↔ tagger ↔ (proprietary link) headset; tagger → IR → other tagger; USB console on the side.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §1, §7c, §7r\nFrame anatomy(render as an annotated string, not bits) +  + ( + token)* + \n\nASCII, comma-delimited tokens. Starts with , ends with .\nEmpty tokens are legal and meaningful — consecutive commas mean \"leave unchanged / not applicable\".  (one empty token) is a different command from .\nExample:  → reply .\nTokens may not contain a comma,  or a line break (that is the validator in : ).\nNotifications can arrive merged (); split on the next  as well as on .\n\nSource: protocol/brx-protocol.md §2, §7e; mcp/brx_mcp/protocol.py\nDEV-03diagram pendingAnnotated frame anatomy.Source: protocol/brx-protocol.md §2\nWaking a gun and holding the link\nConnect and subscribe to the TX notify characteristic. Establishing a link succeeds roughly 1 attempt in 3 (the official app behaves the same); retry — that is the fix.\nOn a fresh power-up, send  then  (after a power-cycle  alone wakes it). A just-booted gun ignores a bare  until then.\n opens the event tap: the gun says \"phone connected\", answers , streams button events and  telemetry, and locks its on-gun menu until a game is configured or it is power-cycled.\nIdle taggers are silent: outside app mode no unsolicited messages are sent — no button, trigger or hit traffic.\nThe official app's connect ritual (captured, fw v4.32) is  →  →  →  (\"connection established\"), then once per session  + . It never sends .\nThe headset must be linked or the gun will connect, answer a quick , then drop within seconds and echo nothing to config. After a gun-initiated , back off ≥ 5 s before reconnecting.\n\nSource: protocol/brx-protocol.md §7a, §7b, §7m, §7r; docs/gotchas.md\nThe safety model.Three layers, in order of what they protect: (1) firmware is never written, so a power-cycle always restores a tagger; (2) a host should refuse malformed frames and require an explicit confirm for any command outside the known-safe list (below); (3) the panic sequence  then  returns a gun to a sane state. Battle Company's official USB updater is the factory-restore path.Source: README.md \"Safety\", protocol/brx-protocol.md §8, mcp/brx_mcp/protocol.py\n\nSource: mcp/brx_mcp/protocol.py\nVolume. is kind to ears on a bench but measurably inaudible for weapon and game audio;  is barely audible. Use 69 (the iOS app's value) for real play.Source: CLAUDE.md hard rules; protocol/brx-protocol.md §3 ($VOL)\nIs the baud rate real over BLE?No — BLE has no baud. 115200 is the UART behind the radio bridge, which is why BLE and a wire speak identical frames.\nWhy does my client drop at ~6.6 s?The link holds fine once up (80 s+ sessions with the official app, multi-minute sessions with ours). Establishment is intermittent; retry in a loop. If it dies within seconds and echoes nothing to config, the headset is not linked.\nCan a command brick the gun?Nothing in the protocol writes firmware. Every state written over BLE is wiped by a power-cycle (except , which persists).\nSource: protocol/brx-protocol.md §7c · protocol/brx-protocol.md §7b, §7e, §7r · protocol/brx-protocol.md §7r, docs/experiment-log.md (2026-08-24 $NAME)\n\n\n  "}},"afterSnapshot":"after@call@143"}
